    "created_at": "2017-05-17T15:15:06Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-05-17T15:28:43Z",
    "body": "By the way, as far as workarounds go: the polyfill I provided might need some updating for more modern APIs (`customElements.define`, object rather than string as second `document.createElement` argument). And it won't work in Firefox without modifying browser configuration to allow custom elements. For now I guess I'll try the mutation observer you shared.\r\n\r\nETA: After testing I've found this (far simpler) solution, using a `MutationObserver`, produces the very same behavior as my earlier example (and in Firefox, too):\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nconst loopchange = new Event('loopchange');\r\n\r\nfunction createHTMLAudioElementWithLoopEvent () {\r\n  const audio = document.createElement('audio');\r\n  new MutationObserver(() => {\r\n    audio.dispatchEvent(loopchange);\r\n  }).observe(audio, {\r\n    attributeFilter: ['loop']\r\n  });\r\n  return audio;\r\n}\r\n```",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
